\ Formatter driver for benchmarking and profiling: formats the files named on
\ the command line and prints the byte length of each result. Run with
\ ONEZ_STDLIB pointed at the standard library so the formatter's own `use`
\ imports resolve at runtime.
\
\ `format-file` rather than `run-fmt`: the profile table shares stdout with the
\ program, so a mode that printed the formatted source would bury it, and the
\ `check:` mode reads every file twice. This is the same work `1z fmt
\ --engine=1z` does per file, minus the writing.
\
\ The lengths are what makes a mode's run comparable to another's. They catch a
\ mode that raised or that formatted to a different size; they do not prove two
\ modes produced the same bytes.
use "formatter" ;
command-line-args [ format-file #byte-len >string print-line ] #each
